#problem()[
  Let $R$ be a noetherian ring. We have shown (or will show) that $X = "Spec"(R)$ can be written as the union of finitely many irreducible closed subsets, $X = X_1 union X_2 union dots.c union X_m$, such that $X_i$ is not contained in $X_j$ for any $i != j$. Show that such a decomposition of $X$ is unique up to reordering the $X_i$'s. It is called the _decomposition of $X$ into its irreducible components_.
]
#solution[
  Assume the contrary, $A_1 union ... union A_a$ and $B_1 union ... union B_b$ are two decompositions of $X$ into irreducible. Note the following that $ A_i = union.big_(j = 1)^(b) A_i inter B_j $ and that $A_i inter B_j$ is closed. Assume the contrary that $S = {j in [b] : A_i inter B_j != emptyset}$ doesn't have only 1 element. $abs(S) != 0 => A_i = emptyset$ which is not true. So $abs(S) > 1$. Thus $ A_i & = union.big_(j in S) A_i inter B_j, $ but this contradicts the assumption that $A_i$ is irreducible. Thus by contradiction, $exists j_i in [b]$ such that $A_i subset.eq B_(j_i)$. Let $phi: [a] -> [b]$ where $i -> j_i$.

  By a similar argument, there is a $psi: [b] -> [a]$ where $j -> i_j$ such that $B_j subset.eq A_(j_i).$

  Assume the contrary, that $psi(phi(i)) = k != i$. Thus $A_i subset.eq B_(phi(i)) subset.eq A_(k)$. This contradicts the maximality of $A_i$, meaning $psi compose phi = 1_([a])$. By a symmetric argument, $phi compose psi = 1_[b].$ Moreover this tells us that $ A_i subset.eq B_(phi(i)) subset.eq A_i subset.eq B_(phi(i)) => A_i = B_(phi(i)). $ meaning $phi$ is just a permutation.
]

#problem()[
  Write $AA^3_CC$ for affine 3-space over $CC$, meaning $"Spec" CC[x, y, z]$. Let $X$ be the closed subset ${x^2 = y z, x z = z} subset AA^3_CC$. (As always, this means the subset where both equations hold.) Decompose $X$ into its irreducible components.
]
#solution[
  $
    X & = {x^2 - y z = 0, x z - z = 0} \
      & = {x^2 - y z = 0, z(x - 1) = 0} \
      & = {x^2 - y z = 0, z = 0} union {x^2 - y z = 0, x - 1 = 0} \
      & = {x^2 = 0, z = 0} union {y z - 1 = 0, x - 1 = 0} \
      & = {x^2 = 0, z = 0} union {y z = 1, x = 1} \
      & = {x = 0, z = 0} union {y z = 1, x = 1}
  $

